  "notes": "LaFace male soloist, largely self-produced, plus Mike City's biggest ballad, 1996–2002. The research names \"two clear modes — a bouncy 95–103 straight kit with the backbeat on 2&4, swung 16th hats and a clap layer; and a 64–71 half-time bed\" — the bouncy half is encoded because five of the eight sourced tempos sit at or above 81 and it is the half whose kit is described in most detail. Three drum deltas: the swung 16th hat (0.60 swing, continuous, 0.54 fill density with the open hat on both offbeats), a clap stack on the backbeat with ghost activity on `2e` and `4a`, and a kick carrying the genre's and-of-4 habit harder than the parent at 0.35. \"Mike City's contribution is a documented drum kit borrowed from Dr. Dre's library\" — a sample-source fact with no schema field, recorded here. Harmony follows City's stated method, \"chords that make the artist feel good\" — 4-chord m7/maj7 cycles with a Wurlitzer or Rhodes lead — so every family runs four deep and the triad share is 0.16. \"Light tenor with layered falsetto self-harmony, moderate note-density\" is the counter at −12 with `octave_echo` sharing top weight, which is what self-harmony means here. The bass is \"plucked synth bass in 16ths on the up-tempos\", so `mirror_kick` with no `bass808` block. ⚠ The research marks the key claim `[LOW-CONF]`. ⚠ Every velocity and density number and `swing.amount` 0.60 is INFERRED. ⚠ `session.keys` is INFERRED: the entry's Keys line gives \"minor-mode with maj7 colour [LOW-CONF]\" and enumerates no tonic, so the values here and their weights are this author's reading of that line.",
